[Music]  mass vaccinations  eased restrictions  and reopened borders  if 2021 was the year the world turned  the tide against covid19  what's to come in 2022  year the economist turns its unrivaled  skill at understanding the future to the  next 12 months  led by editor of the world ahead tom  standage our experts predict the key  trends and stories in the coming year  here's tom with a personal preview of  some of next year's biggest themes  we sort of hope that we'll go from  pandemic to endemic in 2022 and that the  the the coronavirus will become a sort  of manageable disease that we can live  with and we've we've started to get to  get to that position in some western  countries with high levels of  vaccination the real challenge is going  to be to get vaccination up everywhere  not just in the rich world and the  omicron  variant is a reminder that you know as  the the head of the who likes to say no  one is safe until everyone is safe and  we've also got these new therapies  showing up in 2022 antiviral pills and  that again is a very important weapon  against um the coronavirus in general  but also against omicron because um  those antivirals work by suppressing the  replication of rna that's what  viruses rrna and they essentially hijack  the cell in the machinery to copy  themselves and these pills interfere  with that process and that will mean  that they still work against omicron  2022 is i think the year when we're  really going to have to start figuring  out the rules for hybrid working it  turns out women and men involved in  child care are less keen on going back  to the office because it's harder to  juggle work and child care and it also  turns out there are racial disparities  as well white workers are much more  likely to say that they want to go back  to the office than people of color  there's an amazing survey that slack did  that found that three percent of of  information workers of black information  workers said they wanted to go back to  the office full-time versus 21 of white  information workers that was in america  um because maybe offices aren't such  welcoming places if you're not white and  a manager so the worrying possibility  here is that the sort of young white men  all go back to the office they get all  the face time with the managers they get  promoted um they get the pay rises and  that you know the world ends up going  backwards on things like gender pay gaps  and diversity in the workplace  well it's been a lot of enthusiasm for  decentralized finance for crypto uh for  blockchains and so on in uh in 2021 and  it's starting to be taken more seriously  uh by you know mainstream banks and  companies and regulators and so on i  think more broadly what we're seeing  here is the emergence of three sort of  rival visions for the future of finance  the future of money the future of  banking  we've got the sort of tech industry view  of this  so you know companies like facebook you  know trying to launch their own  currencies then you've got the  decentralized finance blockchain crypto  crowd who say well the problem with  those big tech companies is that they're  very centralized  and you know they're too powerful and  then you've got the central bank saying  well hang on a sec if someone's going to  issue digital currencies around here  shouldn't it be us so i think we're  going to see this sort of three-way  battle between these three different  visions and i think in some form or  another we are going to see crypto being  sort of domesticated we've seen this  with with previous technologies that you  know the uh the radical vision is not  quite what we end up with in the end we  get some sort of compromise between the  new way of doing things and the old way  of doing things  we've got a number of space races going  on at the same time here uh in 2021 we  saw three american companies sort of  fighting to um to be the first to put  more tourists into space so we saw  virgin galactic blue origin and spacex  all sent tourists to space what we're  expecting to see in 2022 is more  launches from those companies spacex's  new rocket  called starship which is going to  probably do its first orbital flight in  january or february if that works that  really does change the economics both  for space tourism but also for um this  sort of great power uh rivalry because  america wants to go back to the moon to  remind everybody actually it's top dog  in space and it's it's hoping to use  spacex's technology for part of that and  in particular this starship as the  lander  well i think the general consensus is  that cop 26 you know was a rather  disappointing outcome i mean at least  things didn't go backwards but things  didn't go forward very quickly either a  lot of companies are sort of stepping up  and saying well if governments aren't  going to do things then we're going to  make big you know bold gestures or big  bold sort of promises that we're going  to cut our own emissions we have a piece  by marjun who is a leading chinese  environmentalist in the world ahead  annual and he's saying well the obvious  way for the west and china to cooperate  on this would be for western companies  who do a lot of their manufacturing in  china um to sort of make sure that  they're choosing the lowest carbon  providers in china so his ngo has  produced this database of 10 million  companies in china  and sort of you know how  carbon friendly they are so the idea is  that western companies when they're  choosing suppliers in china can choose  the lowest carbon providers and that  will then lead naturally to competition  between those providers to to reduce  their carbon footprint so i hope that  there will be you know scope for the  private sector to step up here given  that we're not really seeing  the kind of progress we'd like to see  from from governments  thank you for watching to read more of  our coverage of the themes to watch in  2022 click the link and don't forget to  subscribe  [Music]  you

